<worksheet>
  <title>Recitation 2</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions: fill in the appropriate answers in the spaces below.</alert>
    </p>

    <p>
        Clearly write first and last names of each member on the top of the pages.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        We will use the Squeeze Theorem to show
        <md> \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \sqrt{x^3+x^2}\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) = 0. </md>
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find functions <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> such that
                <md> f(x) \leq \sqrt{x^3+x^2}\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \leq g(x). </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>f(x) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                    <mrow>g(x) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                To apply the Squeeze Theorem we need <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x) = \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}g(x).</m>
                Calculate
                <md>
                    \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x) \text{ and } \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}g(x).
                </md>
                (If <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x) \neq \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}g(x)</m> go back and choose different <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>.)
            </p>
            <p> Put your work for calculating <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0}f(x)</m> and <m> \lim_{x\rightarrow 0}g(x)</m> in the space below.</p>
        </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Apply the Squeeze Theorem to calculate <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \sqrt{x^3+x^2}\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right).</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
    <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this exercise you will use the Intermediate Value Theorem to study the roots of polynomials.
        </p>
    </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the Intermediate Value Theorem to show that the function
                    <md> f(x) = x^3 - 5x^2 +x -11</md>
                    has a root.
                    That is, show that there is a solution to <m>f(x) = 0</m>.
                    (You do not have to find the root).
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>p(x)</m> be any polynomial of degree <m>3</m>.
                    Explain why <m>p(x)</m> must have at least one root.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
                <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>p(x)</m> be any polynomial of odd degree.
                    Explain why <m>p(x)</m> must have at least one root.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Does the same argument work for polynomials of even degree?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>